25. Economic Performance of Developing Countries: India, Brazil, East Asia and China
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: I | Topic: Economic performance of developing countries (case study of India, Brazil, East Asian countries and China); overview of the world economy
Introduction
Developing countries show very different outcomes. Comparing India, Brazil, East Asia and China highlights the role of policy, institutions and global integration.
India
Post-1947: planned, inward-looking development (Mahalanobis strategy, import substitution, licence raj) gave a slow "Hindu rate of growth" of about 3.5 per cent. After the 1991 reforms (liberalisation, privatisation, globalisation), growth rose to 6-7 per cent. Strengths: services-led growth, IT, a strong democracy and a demographic dividend. Weaknesses: low manufacturing share, informality, poor health and education outcomes, inequality and weak job creation.
Brazil
Import-substitution industrialisation from the 1930s achieved rapid growth to 1980 (the "Brazilian miracle"), financed by external debt. The 1980s debt crisis and hyperinflation caused the "lost decade". The Real Plan (1994) stabilised prices; the 2000s commodity boom and Bolsa Familia reduced poverty. Since 2014, recession and low productivity have limited growth. High inequality, commodity dependence and premature deindustrialisation persist.
East Asian economies
Japan, then the Four Tigers (Korea, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore) and later ASEAN achieved sustained growth of 7-9 per cent for decades. Features (World Bank's East Asian Miracle, 1993): high savings and investment, export orientation, strong education and human capital, land reform, macroeconomic stability, and an interventionist developmental state (credit allocation, industrial policy, performance-based support). The 1997 Asian crisis exposed financial vulnerabilities.
China
Since 1978 reforms (household responsibility system, special economic zones, state-enterprise reform, WTO entry in 2001), China averaged nearly 10 per cent growth for four decades and lifted hundreds of millions out of poverty. Drivers: very high investment, export manufacturing, FDI, gradualism and state capacity. Challenges: debt, demographic ageing, inequality, property sector stress and slowing growth.
